!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/32/app/orders-api-3.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The orders service described as types, in one ordinary schema.

  The whole header is the ordinary one -- meta.tn governing, core.tn imported -- so this needs no
  meta layer, no kernel import, no `top`, and no annotations. Everything below is a plain type.

  HOW THE METADATA IS CARRIED. A FIXED field (§5.2's `= value`) puts a value in the type where an
  annotation would have put it beside the type. `method: http_method = POST` is checked by the
  resolver, survives into resolver output, and cannot drift from what the operation is -- where an
  annotation's value would have been silently dropped unless its type lived in the governing
  meta-schema.

  HOW A RESPONSE IS CARRIED. A response is one of several, which is what `choice` is for. Each
  variant fixes its own status, so a reader of the schema can see which status carries which body.

  The variants are NOT proved disjoint, and need not be: §5.4 requires distinct variant types, not
  disjoint value sets, and record-set disjointness is a case a resolver may leave unproven. So a
  response value carries its variant tag -- `!order_created { ... }` -- which is the honest outcome
  rather than a wrinkle: the tag names the response, and nothing has to be inferred from structure.

  WHAT AN OPERATION VALUE IS. Because an operation is an ordinary record, a value of one is a real
  thing: an exchange. { method: POST  path: "/orders"  request: <an order>  response: <a response> }
  is one request and its reply. The same declaration serves as the contract and as the shape of a
  trace or an access log, which no data-shaped description can do.

  HOW THE SHAPE IS ENFORCED. `operation` is a base record every operation composes (§5.8), so the
  resolver checks each one has a method and a path, and an operation is recognisable in resolved
  output by its supertype rather than by a naming convention. Composition narrows: an operation
  inherits `method` and re-declares it FIXED to its own verb.

  What the base cannot state is that a response is one -- each operation's choice has different
  variants -- so `response` is still convention. A type constructor would close that last gap; see
  sketch/README.md.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  order => { sku: non_empty_text  quantity: int32 }

  @doc:"Abbreviated; the shipping one is problem-2.tn, which follows RFC 9457."
  problem => { type: uri?  title: text  status: status_code  detail: text? }

  @doc:"A business error: schema-valid, and the domain still said no. Composes problem (§5.8)."
  sku_not_found => problem & { missing_sku: non_empty_text }

  @doc:"Every operation composes this, so an operation is found by its supertype, not by its name."
  operation => { method: http_method  path: text }

  order_created  => { status: status_code = 201  body: order }
  order_invalid  => { status: status_code = 400  body: problem }
  order_sku_gone => { status: status_code = 404  body: sku_not_found }

  @doc:"Accept an order and confirm it."
  create_order => operation & {
    method:   http_method = POST
    path:     text = "/orders"
    request:  order
    response: (order_created | order_invalid | order_sku_gone)
  }

  schema_served  => { status: status_code = 200 }
  schema_missing => { status: status_code = 404  body: problem }

  @doc:"Fetch a published schema. A schema document is not a TSON value, so schema_served has no body."
  @doc:"A record at a type position is not permitted (§5.2), so a parameter set is a named type."
  get_schema_parameters => { schema_path: non_empty_text }

  get_schema => operation & {
    method:     http_method = GET
    path:       text = "/schemas/{schema_path}"
    parameters: get_schema_parameters
    response:   (schema_served | schema_missing)
  }
}
